<h2 id="u3">Unit 3: Plate Tectonics &amp; Natural Hazards <span class="quiet-small quiet-regular">(18 terms)</span></h2>

<h3 id="plate-tectonic">Plate (tectonic)</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> A large segment of Earth's outer shell (lithosphere) that moves slowly over the asthenosphere below. ~15 major plates cover Earth.</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> A jigsaw-puzzle map of Earth with major plates outlined.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "The Pacific Plate subducts beneath the Okhotsk Plate near Japan, causing megathrust earthquakes."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">தட்டு (tectonic)</td><td lang="hi">विवर्तनी प्लेट</td><td lang="fr">plaque tectonique</td><td lang="ko">판 (tectonic)</td><td lang="ja">プレート（地殻）</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="convergent-boundary">Convergent boundary</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> Where two plates push toward each other. Produces earthquakes, mountain building (Himalayas), or subduction (volcanic arcs).</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> Two arrows pointing toward each other; one plate diving below.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "Japan sits on a convergent boundary where the Pacific Plate dives beneath the Okhotsk Plate."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">ஒருங்கிணைவு எல்லை / convergent boundary</td><td lang="hi">अभिसारी सीमा</td><td lang="fr">frontière convergente</td><td lang="ko">수렴형 경계</td><td lang="ja">収束境界</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="divergent-boundary">Divergent boundary</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> Where two plates pull apart. Produces mid-ocean ridges, sea-floor spreading, rift valleys.</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> Two arrows pointing away from each other; magma rising in the gap.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is a divergent boundary creating new ocean floor."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">பிரிவு எல்லை / divergent boundary</td><td lang="hi">अपसारी सीमा</td><td lang="fr">frontière divergente</td><td lang="ko">발산형 경계</td><td lang="ja">発散境界</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="transform-boundary">Transform boundary</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> Where two plates slide past each other horizontally. Produces strike-slip earthquakes (like Kobe 1995, San Andreas).</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> Two plates with horizontal arrows in opposite directions; sliding past.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "The Anatolian Fault under Istanbul is a transform boundary; like San Andreas, it produces shaking but no tsunamis."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">மாற்றமைப்பு எல்லை / transform boundary</td><td lang="hi">रूपांतर सीमा</td><td lang="fr">frontière transformante</td><td lang="ko">변환형 경계</td><td lang="ja">トランスフォーム境界</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="subduction">Subduction</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> When one plate dives beneath another at a convergent boundary. Produces megathrust earthquakes, tsunamis, stratovolcanoes.</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> Cross-section showing oceanic plate sliding under continental plate.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "The Tōhoku 2011 megathrust earthquake was caused by subduction of the Pacific Plate."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">உட்செலுத்துதல் (subduction)</td><td lang="hi">अवतलन (subduction)</td><td lang="fr">subduction</td><td lang="ko">섭입 (subduction)</td><td lang="ja">沈み込み（subduction）</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="megathrust">Megathrust</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> A very large earthquake at a subduction zone, where the entire interface ruptures. Largest earthquakes on Earth (M9+).</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> A subduction zone with the locked interface suddenly slipping.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "Tōhoku 2011 (M9.1) was one of the largest megathrust events ever recorded."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">மெகாத்ரஸ்ட் / பெரும்-தள்ளுதல்</td><td lang="hi">महा-थ्रस्ट</td><td lang="fr">mégathrust / méga-séisme</td><td lang="ko">메가스러스트 지진</td><td lang="ja">メガスラスト地震</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="tsunami">Tsunami</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> A series of very long waves caused by vertical displacement of the seafloor (usually from a megathrust earthquake).</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> A wave traveling across ocean, reaching coast as a wall of water.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami killed ~230,000 people across 14 countries."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
