<h3 id="intermittency">Intermittency</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> When energy generation isn't constant, solar doesn't shine at night, wind doesn't blow on demand. The central challenge for renewables.</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> A graph of solar generation: high midday, zero at night.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "Intermittency creates the need for energy storage + flexible grids."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">முற்றுப்படா மாறுபாடு / intermittency</td><td lang="hi">आंतरायिकता / असंतत्ता</td><td lang="fr">intermittence</td><td lang="ko">간헐성 / 비연속성</td><td lang="ja">間欠性・出力変動</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="energy-storage">Energy storage</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> Storing electricity to use later. Includes batteries, pumped hydro, hydrogen, thermal storage. Critical for renewable integration.</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> A battery bank or pumped-hydro reservoir storing energy.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "Energy storage costs have dropped 70% in a decade, enabling more renewable integration."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">சக்தி சேமிப்பு</td><td lang="hi">ऊर्जा भंडारण</td><td lang="fr">stockage d'énergie</td><td lang="ko">에너지 저장</td><td lang="ja">エネルギー貯蔵</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="transition-energy">Transition (energy)</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> The shift from one energy system to another, typically from fossil fuels to renewables. Takes decades and major investment.</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> A coal plant being replaced by solar + wind + storage over time.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "India's energy transition will take decades; net-zero by 2070 is the national commitment."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">சக்தி மாற்றம் / transition</td><td lang="hi">ऊर्जा रूपांतरण</td><td lang="fr">transition énergétique</td><td lang="ko">에너지 전환</td><td lang="ja">エネルギー転換</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="decarbonization">Decarbonization</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> Reducing carbon emissions across the economy. Different from net-zero (which includes removals): focuses on cutting emissions.</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> A graph of emissions trending downward over time.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "Decarbonization requires changes across electricity + industry + transport + buildings + agriculture."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">கார்பன் குறைப்பு</td><td lang="hi">डीकार्बोनाइज़ेशन / कार्बन कमी</td><td lang="fr">décarbonation</td><td lang="ko">탈탄소화</td><td lang="ja">脱炭素化</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="carbon-accounting">Carbon accounting</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> Measuring emissions across an organization. Scope 1 = direct emissions; Scope 2 = electricity purchased; Scope 3 = supply chain.</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> Three concentric circles labeled Scope 1, 2, 3.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "Companies often report Scope 1+2 emissions; Scope 3 (supply chain) is harder to measure but often largest."</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tamil</th><th>Hindi</th><th>Français</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody><tr><td lang="ta">கார்பன் கணக்கீடு</td><td lang="hi">कार्बन लेखांकन</td><td lang="fr">comptabilité carbone</td><td lang="ko">탄소 회계 / 탄소 산정</td><td lang="ja">カーボン会計・炭素計算</td></tr></tbody>
</table>

<h3 id="op-ed">Op-Ed</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> An opinion-editorial article, a short, audience-aware argument piece for general newspapers or magazines. Different from academic essay.</p>
<p><strong>Visual cue:</strong> A newspaper page with an opinion column highlighted.</p>
<p><strong>In a sentence:</strong> "Your Op-Ed should sound like a published newspaper op-ed, not like a school essay."</p>
